#edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186053226 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186053226 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "92959582-544d-51a2-9941-734879b6e119" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186053227 #edb/ref 1080 "0.12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186053227 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186053227 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "27190c4d-66f9-539b-b083-e122239eba9e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186053228 #edb/ref 1080 "0.52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186053228 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186053228 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5434afcf-de9a-5ac6-a3f1-0a118a94fca5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054551 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5104" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054551 #edb/ref 1071 "In a class, each student selected one course among the two possible options, math or physics. The results are given in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Math | Physics | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Female | $12$ | $12$ | $24$ |\n| Male | $20$ | $6$ | $26$ |\n| Totals | $32$ | $18$ | $50$ |\n\nEstimate the probability that a randomly chosen student selected physics or is male (or both)." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054551 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "671da5f4-e539-588e-9bec-81c71e016d2f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054551 #edb/ref 1074 "To estimate the probability that a randomly chosen student selected physics or is male, we\n\n- add up the numbers that are in the column **\"Physics\"** or the row **\"Male\"**, and then\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe numbers in the column **\"Physics\"** or the row **\"Male\"** are highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Math | Physics | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Female | $12$ | $\\color{blue}12$ | $24$ |\n| Male | $\\color{blue}20$ | $\\color{blue}6$ | $26$ |\n| Totals | $32$ | $18$ | $50$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(50)$, we get\n\n$$\np =\\dfrac{12+20+6}{50} = \\dfrac{38}{50} =0.76\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054552 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5105" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054552 #edb/ref 1071 "Anna surveys some students in a school, asking them what pets out of cats or dogs they have at home. Her findings are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Has a dog | Doesn't have a dog | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Has a cat | $30$ | $56$ | $86$ |\n| Doesn't have a cat | $60$ | $54$ | $114$ |\n| Totals | $90$ | $110$ | $200$ |\n\nLet $C$ be the event that a randomly selected student in the school **\"Has a cat\"**, and $D$ be the event that a randomly selected student in the school **\"Has a dog\"**. Estimate $P(C \\cap D)$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054552 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ec0a90ec-7cd7-5ab5-abb2-9e33cbd4b711" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054552 #edb/ref 1074 "The event $C \\cap D$ is the event that a randomly selected student in the school has both a cat and a dog.\n\nTo estimate the probability of $C \\cap D$, we need to\n\n- find the number at the intersection of the row **\"Has a cat\"** and the column **\"Has a dog\"**, and then\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe number at the intersection of the row **\"Has a cat\"** and the column **\"Has a dog\"** is highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Has a dog | Doesn't have a dog | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Has a cat | $\\color{blue}30$ | $56$ | $86$ |\n| Doesn't have a cat | $60$ | $54$ | $114$ |\n| Totals | $90$ | $110$ | $200$ |\n\nDividing by the total number of data points in the table $(200)$, we get\n\n$$\nP(C \\cap D) = \\dfrac{30}{200} = 0.15\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054553 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5106" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054553 #edb/ref 1071 "There are $250$ boys and $250$ girls in a school. Of them, $40$ boys and $40$ girls were randomly selected and asked which sport they prefer between volleyball, basketball, football, or soccer. The results of the survey are given in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Volleyball | Basketball | Football | Soccer | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Girl | $14$ | $10$ | $2$ | $14$ | $40$ |\n| Boy | $8$ | $12$ | $11$ | $9$ | $40$ |\n| Totals | $22$ | $22$ | $13$ | $23$ | $80$ |\n\nEstimate the probability that a randomly selected student in the survey is a boy who prefers football or soccer, and then use this to estimate the number of students in the entire school who are boys that prefer football or soccer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054553 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f09f68d2-f44f-5328-963b-41a9884a944a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054553 #edb/ref 1074 "To estimate the probability that a randomly selected student is a boy who prefers football or soccer, we\n\n- add up the numbers at the intersection of the row **\"Boy\"** and the columns **\"Football\"** and **\"Soccer\"**, and then\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe numbers at the intersection of the row **\"Boy\"** and the columns **\"Football\"** and **\"Soccer\"** are highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Volleyball | Basketball | Football | Soccer | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Girl | $14$ | $10$ | $2$ | $14$ | $40$ |\n| Boy | $8$ | $12$ | $\\color{blue}11$ | $\\color{blue}9$ | $40$ |\n| Totals | $22$ | $22$ | $13$ | $23$ | $80$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(80)$, we get\n\n$$\np = \\dfrac{11+9}{80} = \\dfrac{20}{80} = 0.25\n$$\n\nThis represents the estimated probability that a randomly selected student is a boy who prefers football or soccer.\n\nFinally, to estimate the number of students in the entire school who are boys that prefer football or soccer, we multiply the total number of students in the school $(500)$ by the probability above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= 500 \\cdot p \\\\\n&= 500 \\cdot 0.25 \\\\\n&= 125\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054661 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186055331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054661 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186055338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054661 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054661 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054661 #edb/ref 1039 "Estimating the Probability of the Union of Events" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054661 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186054551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054661 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "ac12981c-ec1d-5f2d-8699-a03bd7d85e14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054662 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054662 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186055328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054662 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186055330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054662 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054662 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054662 #edb/ref 1039 "Estimating the Probability of the Intersection of Events" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054662 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186054552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054662 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "46546a09-5d48-57d9-8166-5f9d8949c066" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054663 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186055329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054663 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186055339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054663 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054663 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054663 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054663 #edb/ref 1039 "Estimating Using Probabilities" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054663 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186054553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054663 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b6956b58-5581-5221-8d4f-88ebde382f0c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054881 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186055614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054881 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186055615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054881 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186055616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054881 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186055617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054881 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186055663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054881 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054881 #edb/ref 1045 "Compound Events in Probability From Experimental Data" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054881 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186055614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054881 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054881 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "c3665ee3-8127-5934-8cf7-8043de2f83a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055329 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186053211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055329 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47138" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055329 #edb/ref 1071 "A group of randomly selected students from School A was asked about whether or not they use TV or the internet to access the local news. The results of the survey are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | TV | No TV | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Internet | $90$ | $15$ | $105$ |\n| No internet | $30$ | $15$ | $45$ |\n| Totals | $120$ | $30$ | $150$ |\n\nThere are $100$ students in School B. Assuming that the distribution of the results is the same for both schools,\nestimate the number of School B students that access the local news either from TV or the internet, but not both.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055329 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aa1f968b-ce45-555d-acfc-8866cb45d38d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055329 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055331 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186053223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055331 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47141" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055331 #edb/ref 1071 "In a high school, a group of students was asked about whether they prefer apples, oranges, or bananas. The results are given in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Apple | Orange | Banana | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Girls | $11$ | $8$ | $7$ | $26$ |\n| Boys | $12$ | $6$ | $6$ | $24$ |\n| Totals | $23$ | $14$ | $13$ | $50$ |\n\nEstimate the probability that a randomly chosen student in the group preferred apples or bananas or is a girl.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055331 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "66372d89-3abf-597d-98d0-70064323b74c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055331 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055614 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055614 #edb/ref 1061 18718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055614 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e7ae61c2-30a3-54cf-88c5-c1bbc9d77e99" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055614 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186055977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055615 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055615 #edb/ref 1061 18719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055615 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d567d447-5c82-574d-9c7c-79e70871c3e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055615 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055616 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055616 #edb/ref 1061 18720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055616 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "52d226ea-739d-53c5-8fc5-a4d45df4a932" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055616 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055617 #edb/ref 1061 18721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055617 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c37ad3ca-2c39-5b1c-849a-72d3c40e8f36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055617 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055663 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055663 #edb/ref 1061 28504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055663 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cd4e2ae0-2688-5445-855e-0aa2d0de7087" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055663 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186055990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055977 #edb/ref 1064 "Sometimes, we want to find the probability that two or more events co-occur (occur together). Events like this, involving a combination of two or more events, are called **compound events**.\n\nFor instance, the $100$ members of a volleyball club were asked about their preferences between Chinese food and Italian pizza. The results of the survey are represented in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Chinese food | Italian pizza | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Male | $20$ | $35$ | $55$ |\n| Female | $20$ | $25$ | $45$ |\n| Totals | $40$ | $60$ | $100$ |\n\nSuppose that we want to find the probability that a randomly selected club member is a male that prefers pizza.\n\nFirst, let $A$ be the event a randomly selected member is **\"Male\"** and let $B$ be the event that a randomly selected member prefers **\"Italian pizza\"**.\n\nThe probability that $A$ *and* $B$ co-occur, denoted by $P(A\\cap B)$ and called the **intersection**, is found by identifying the intersection of these events in the table.\n\nThe number at the intersection of the column **\"Italian pizza\"** and the row **\"Male\"** is highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Chinese food | Italian pizza | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Male | $20$ | $\\color{blue}35$ | $55$ |\n| Female | $20$ | $25$ | $45$ |\n| Totals | $40$ | $60$ | $100$ |\n\nDividing this number by the total number of data points in the table $(100)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(A∩B) &= \\frac{35}{100} \\\\\n&= 0.35\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents the experimental probability that a randomly selected member prefers Italian pizza and is male." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055977 #edb/ref 1065 3364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055977 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5feb6681-bfdb-5bfb-9230-a4f34bbd80ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055977 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055990 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's return to our first example, where the Volleyball club members were asked about whether they preferred Italian pizza or Chinese food. The data is shown below.\n\n|  | Chinese food | Italian pizza | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Male | $20$ | $35$ | $55$ |\n| Female | $20$ | $25$ | $45$ |\n| Totals | $40$ | $60$ | $100$ |\n\nSuppose we want to find the probability that a randomly selected club member either prefers Chinese food *or* is female (or both).\n\nLet $A$ be the event that a randomly selected member prefers **\"Chinese food\"** and let $B$ be the event that a randomly selected member is **\"Female\"**.\n\nThe probability that $A$ *or* $B$ (or both) occurs, denoted by $P(A\\cup B)$ and called the **union**, is found by counting the number of data points in which $A$ or $B$ occurred and dividing by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe numbers that are in *either* the column **\"Chinese food\"** *or* the row **\"Female\"** (or both) are highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Chinese food | Italian pizza | Totals |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Male | $\\color{blue}20$ | $35$ | $55$ |\n| Female | $\\color{blue}20$ | $\\color{blue}25$ | $45$ |\n| Totals | $40$ | $60$ | $100$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(100)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(A∪B) &= \\frac{20 + 20 + 25}{100} \\\\\n&= \\frac{65}{100} \\\\\n&= 0.65\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents the experimental probability that a randomly selected member prefers Chinese food or is female." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055990 #edb/ref 1065 4631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055990 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "098069a2-2cbc-5491-b9de-52d1e84a32c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055990 #edb/ref 1067 "The Union of Two Events" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234062 #edb/ref 1069 "q-46814" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234062 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234062 #edb/ref 1071 "Martin surveyed some students in a school, asking them if they have cats or dogs at home. His findings are summarized in the two-way frequency table below.\n\n|  | Has a dog | Doesn't have a dog | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Has a cat | $6$ | $9$ | $15$ |\n| Doesn't have a cat | $10$ | $7$ | $17$ |\n| Totals | $16$ | $16$ | $32$ |\n\nEstimate the probability that a randomly chosen student in the school has either a cat or a dog but not both." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234062 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cca7bbd7-fb2c-50b2-babf-c466777230b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234062 #edb/ref 1074 "To estimate the probability that a randomly chosen student in the school has either a cat or a dog but not both, we\n\n- add up the numbers in the row **\" Has a cat\"** or the column **\"Has a dog\"** but not in their intersection, and\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe numbers in the row **\"Has a cat\"** or the column **\"Has a dog\"** but not in their intersection are highlighted below.\n\n|  | Has a dog | Doesn't have a dog | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Has a cat | $6$ | $9$ | $15$ |\n| Doesn't have a cat | $10$ | $7$ | $17$ |\n| Totals | $16$ | $16$ | $32$ |\n\nAdding up these numbers and then dividing by the total number of data points in the table $(32),$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{9+10}{32}=\\frac{19}{32}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
